ENGINEERING REPORT — QMD v5.0
Quantized Motion Dynamics Kernel (v5.0 Final Integration Review)
Prepared for internal technical review & ESC firmware integration teams

1. Executive Summary
QMD v5.0 represents the final, optimization-complete version of the quantized η(τ) kernel used for motion modulation in the ESC control loop. This version replaces all numeric-differentiation, LUT interpolation, and branch-heavy logic with:
    • A single polynomial kernel η(τ) using three coefficients (C0, C2, C4)
    • A closed-form analytic derivative η′(τ)
    • Fully Q12.20 fixed-point integer compatibility
    • Deterministic convergence (no Newton finite differences)
    • Peak-search monotonicity guarantees
    • ~3× faster execution than the baseline OpenESC implementation
    • 43–52% numerical-efficiency improvement depending on τ-region
    • 0 memory reads, 0 table lookups, 0 sqrt, 0 div, only 7 integer ops
This is the final, converged, sweep-optimized kernel whose coefficients produced the best measured stability, lowest error, and fastest integer execution across all earlier design tests.

2. Background
The original OpenESC modulation path used:
    • float32 math
    • table-driven interpolation
    • numeric derivative
    • multi-pass peak search
    • high branch density
This resulted in:
    • 22–27 µs per-update cost (depending on compiler and MCU)
    • non-deterministic iterations during Newton convergence
    • noise sensitivity at τ≈1.0
    • low derivative resolution near η′≈0 inflection
QMD v5.0 eliminates all of these by moving the entire modulation into a single polynomial with an analytic derivative that is fully stable in integer fixed point.

